# EVERY PATTERN SAYS WHICH OF TWO THINGS IT MEANS.
#
# A leading `/` names ONE place, resolved relative to this file. A leading
# `**/` means every depth, on purpose. A pattern with neither behaves exactly
# like `**/` and nobody said so — which is how `coverage/`, written for the
# report directory at this root, came to swallow `src/lib/coverage/`, a live
# source directory the component registry imports unconditionally. The working
# tree built; every fresh clone did not.
#
# Anchoring is what survived the merge, and this file is the proof. It is the
# union of the two roots that became this one: the web tree's root and the
# harness root's. Measured at the assembly: an UNANCHORED `research/` names
# 274 tracked files under `engine/docs/research/`, and an unanchored
# `experiments/` names 80. Each pattern below is therefore anchored to this
# root or marked every-depth on purpose, and each subtree that carries its own
# rules — `engine/`, `bench/`, `tests/performance/` — keeps them, because git
# resolves an anchored pattern relative to the `.gitignore` that declares it.
#
# ONE PATTERN WAS DELIBERATELY NOT CARRIED OVER AT THE ASSEMBLY: the harness
# root's `/experiments/` named a dumping ground at THAT root; at THIS root
# `experiments/` was a TRACKED tree, so importing the rule would have hidden
# live content — the class of defect this header describes. (Its later life
# as an anchored rule here ended with the merge door: see below.)
# `scripts/harness-root/merge-round.mjs` once defaulted its experiments root
# to `<this root>/experiments` and read nothing; it now takes
# `--experiments-root` and has no default, so nothing reads an experiments
# directory unless somebody names it.
#
# THE EXPERIMENT ARCHIVE IS TRACKED, AND NO RULE HERE NAMES IT. The
# experiments-shape decision put the whole experiment archive here:
# `experiments/engine/` (the engine repo's numbered rounds),
# `experiments/web/`, `experiments/archive/` — records, versioned on this
# branch beside the work that produced them. What ships to the product branch
# is the promotion profile's answer (`promotion/testing-to-master.json`
# excludes `experiments/`), never an ignore rule: the anchored rule and its
# re-admission that used to make the archive "floor bytes" retired with the
# merge door (PROMO-4), and so did the product branch's prophylactic copies
# of these rules, which made local bytes expendable on a checkout
# (merge-door-final J13).

# Dependencies and build output. Every-depth on purpose: npm creates nested
# `node_modules`, and build output lands beside whatever produced it.
**/node_modules/
node_modules
**/dist/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Logs land wherever the thing that wrote them runs.
**/*.log

# Harness runtime state (databases, caches). Every-depth: a harness run
# writes one wherever its project root is, including under a fixture.
**/.bridlewise/
# AND EVERY SUFFIXED SIBLING OF IT. State directories are sometimes renamed
# aside with a suffix, so a single literal spelling would let the moved copy
# through. A wildcard covers the next suffix without another hand-maintained
# entry. The rule is deliberately not directory-only: an archive with the same
# name is the same state in a different shape. Never commit databases, keys,
# or state archives.
**/.bridlewise.*

# Local dev scratch and the two browser-driven report directories, all at
# this root and all written by a tool that runs from it.
/.dev/
/test-results/
/playwright-report/

# Coverage report output, written at this root. The original defect, and the
# reason every rule in this file is now explicit one way or the other.
/coverage/

# Transient merge state, written at this root by `scripts/harness-root/merge-round.mjs`.
# Each of these is `resolve(<this root>, …)` in that file: the lock and the
# report at its top, and the two promote temporaries at `dirname(engine)`,
# which IS this root now that the engine is `engine/` rather than a sibling.
#
# `/.staging/` USED TO BE HERE AND WAS NEVER WRITTEN HERE. The staging tree is
# `resolve(integrationDir, ".staging")` — `engine/.staging`, one directory
# down — so the rule at this root defended a place nothing writes while the
# place something does write was unignored, and `--no-e2e` LEAVES that tree in
# place on purpose for inspection. The rule moved to `engine/.gitignore`,
# which is the only file whose anchored spelling can name it.
/.merge.lock
/.integration-staging-tmp/
/.integration-old-promote/
/MERGE-ROUND-REPORT.json
# AND THE MERGE TOOL'S INPUT, WHICH BELONGS ONE DIRECTORY DOWN, NEVER HERE.
# `merge-round.mjs` reads `<experiments-root>/<expNNN-…>/merge-manifest.json`,
# where the root is named on the command line — one level down from whatever
# root it is given, never at a repository root. Five copies were sitting HERE,
# made while somebody was reading them and then committed. Anchored on purpose:
# the every-depth spelling would hide every manifest in the tree, including
# the ones under `experiments/` that record closed rounds and the ones a
# future round will be pointed at. The rule names the wrong PLACE for a file
# whose right place is one directory deeper.
/merge-manifest*.json

# `/.bkp/` — copies kept aside during earlier surgery. It was TRACKED here
# until the clean-root work removed it, which is why the rule could not exist
# before: an ignore rule over a tracked path does nothing, and the moment the
# path is untracked the rule is what stops it coming back. The directory
# itself is still the right place to put a file before mutating it; what is
# wrong is committing what lands there, so the rule ignores rather than
# forbids. (Its sibling of that era, the closed merge rounds' `experiments/`,
# is the tracked experiment archive now: see the header.)
/.bkp/

# Transient workspace-lease state, written at this root.
/.workspace-lease/

# Separate repositories / full forks that may be cloned beside the work but are
# NOT part of it. Tracking them via `git add -A` once pulled 600+ experiment
# repositories in as submodules and bloated the worktree. All anchored to this
# root: each names one place, and `research/` in particular must never go
# every-depth (see the header's measurement).
#
# THESE RULES ARE PROPHYLACTIC, AND MOST OF THEM NAME NOTHING TODAY. That is
# the point: each names a place where a huge foreign tree is known to land, so
# the rule has to be there BEFORE the tree is, not after somebody has already
# run `git add -A`. Do not prune one for being "dead" — `/large-codebases/`
# was raised as dead in an audit on 2026-07-31 because the corpus currently
# lives at `engine/large-codebases` (declared by `engine/.gitignore`, which
# resolves it relative to itself). Both rules are correct and neither is
# redundant: they defend two different places, and either place can be the one
# the next checkout uses.
/candidate/
/large-codebases/
/mutant-tests/
/probes/
/research/
/detect-secrets/

# Raw evidence held in the evidence store, never in git: `harness evidence
# materialize` writes a recorded file back at its recorded path, and the copy
# must never be committed. The block below is GENERATED from the tracked
# evidence manifests (harness sync; never edit it by hand).

# EVERY MOVED-ASIDE SIBLING OF EVERY RULE ABOVE, AS ONE RULE ABOUT THE SHAPE.
#
# `**/.bridlewise.*` two blocks up fixes ONE of these. Every OTHER directory
# rule in this repository had the identical hole: enumerated mechanically,
# 28 of the 31 directory-style rules across the four tracked `.gitignore`
# files did not cover their own moved-aside sibling — `node_modules`, `dist`,
# `/coverage/`,
# `/.staging/`, `/.workspace-lease/`, `/candidate/`, `engine/guardian/build/`,
# `**/__pycache__/`, all of them.
#
# The rename is not an accident anybody could stop doing. It is PRESCRIBED:
# §39/W-08 says preserve state by moving it aside rather than clearing it,
# `mv <dir> <dir>.<label>-$(date +%s)`, and `scripts/testing/sweep-cache-evidence.ts`
# exists to clean up after it. Nothing in this tree writes the label — an
# operator types it — so the next spelling is unknowable and a second literal
# beside the first defends exactly one more of them.
#
# SO THE RULE NAMES THE SHAPE, the way `bench/.gitignore` does for its own
# root, and for the same reason: a list can only defend the names somebody has
# already met. One rule covers all 31, and covers the 32nd the day it is
# written, because nobody has to remember to come back here.
#
# WHAT MAKES A SHAPE RULE SAFE HERE IS THE EPOCH, NOT THE LABEL. A label alone
# (`.aside`, `.bkp`) is a word, and an every-depth rule over a word is the
# `coverage/` defect again — it would swallow a directory somebody means to
# track. Ten or more digits at the end of a name is a timestamp, and nothing in
# a source tree is named after one.
#
# A HIGHER RUNG WAS CONSIDERED AND DECLINED, AND THE REASON IS A MECHANISM.
# If the prescribed rename targeted an ALREADY-IGNORED destination — `mv <dir>
# .bkp/<dir>.<label>` against the `/.bkp/` rule above — no unmatched name would
# ever be produced and this rule could be deleted: the class would be
# unreachable by shape rather than matched. It is declined for three reasons,
# none of which is the size of the change:
#   1. THERE IS NO DOOR TO PUT THE ARGUMENT ON. Nothing in this repository
#      executes the rename; an operator types it into a shell. "Unreachable by
#      shape" needs a call site that demands the right destination, and a
#      convention that everybody must remember is rung 6 wearing rung 3's
#      clothes — the exact substitution the strength ladder exists to refuse.
#   2. IT WOULD BREAK THE ONE CONSUMER THE MOVED-ASIDE NAME HAS.
#      `scripts/testing/sweep-cache-evidence.ts` finds evidence directories by reading
#      `node_modules/` and matching the shape BESIDE the live cache. Sitting
#      where the original sat is what makes a moved-aside directory findable at
#      all; relocating them under `.bkp/` moves them out of that reader's
#      universe, which is a silently smaller scan — this file's own recurring
#      failure.
#   3. `/.bkp/` IS ANCHORED PER ROOT ON PURPOSE (`bench/.gitignore` records
#      why), so the prescription would need one such rule at every root where a
#      rename can happen. That is a list of places again, which is what the
#      shape rule below replaced.
#
# THE RULE USED TO REQUIRE A LITERAL `.` BEFORE THE LABEL (`**/*.*-<epoch>*`)
# AND THAT GAP WAS ARBITRARY, NOT PRINCIPLED. `mv .bridlewise
# .bridlewise-1785461259` was covered — but only because the LEADING DOT of
# the directory's own name happened to satisfy the `*.*`, which has nothing to
# do with the rename. `mv node_modules node_modules-1785461259` and
# `mv engine/guardian/build engine/guardian/build-1785461259` were NOT covered,
# both driven. So the dot defended whichever names happened to contain one, and
# the hyphenated spelling of the rename is the one an operator types when the
# directory has no dot in it — which is most of them.
#
# The dot is gone. Measured at the time of the change, over the union of both
# tracked indexes: 21,290 tracked path segments, ZERO matches. That number is
# not the guard — `tests/prevention/tracked-source-owner.test.ts` case C reds
# on ANY tracked path an ignore rule matches, at any depth and in every root,
# so the day a committed file takes this shape it is a red rather than a stale
# paragraph here.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, and that is measured rather than stylistic — the same
# measurement `engine/.gitignore` records for `/large-codebases`. That path is
# a SYMLINK; a trailing slash makes git match real directories only, so a
# directory-only rule here would miss the moved-aside spelling of exactly the
# entries that need a slashless rule in the first place. It also covers
# `.bridlewise.archived-<epoch>.tar`, which is the same secret in a different
# shape — the reason the `.bridlewise.*` rule above is slashless too.
#
# THE TRAILING `*` IS THE PRICE OF THAT, AND THE PRICE IS NOW MEASURED RATHER
# THAN ASSERTED. It means ten digits followed by ANYTHING match — including
# inside a FILE name, so `book.isbn-9780134685991.json`,
# `data.export-20260731010342.csv` and `app.min-1234567890.js` are all silently
# unaddable by `git add -A`. Zero such names exist in this index today.
#
# THE NARROWER SPELLING WAS TRIED, NOT DISMISSED, AND IT LOSES MORE THAN IT
# SAVES. Dropping the `*` anchors the epoch to the end of the name and clears
# every false positive above — and it stops covering three of the six names the
# rename really produces: both millisecond-epoch spellings and the `.tar`
# archive. The mechanism is `fnmatch`, which git's matcher is: it has NO
# REPETITION OPERATOR, so "and the rest is digits" is inexpressible, `[0-9]*`
# means "one digit then anything", and the epoch's own width is not fixed
# (`date +%s` is ten, milliseconds are thirteen, and the sweeper's `\d+` bounds
# it only from below).
#
# THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO END "there is no spelling in this language that admits
# the six and refuses the three", AND THAT WAS FALSE — measured, not argued.
# THREE anchored rules do it: ten digits, thirteen digits, and ten digits plus
# `.tar`. Driven in a throwaway repository, that set covers all six names the
# rename produces and swallows none of the three file names above. The sentence
# was a claim about a search nobody had run, and it read like a proof.
#
# THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES ON A DIFFERENT MECHANISM, AND THIS FILE ALREADY
# STATES IT TWICE. `fnmatch` has no repetition operator, so a pattern that
# ANCHORS the epoch to the end of the name matches a FIXED number of digit
# positions — which means any candidate that refuses the three false positives
# can only cover the widths it spells out, one rule per width. That is a LIST,
# and the whole reason this rule names a shape is that a list defends only the
# names somebody has already met. It is not a theoretical objection: the
# three-rule set above MISSES eleven-, twelve-, fourteen-, sixteen- and
# nineteen-digit epochs, all driven, and the last two are what `date +%s%6N`
# and `date +%s%N` print — spellings an operator can type today, in a rename
# nothing in this tree writes. The trailing `*` covers every width there will
# ever be, because it never had to know how many there are.
#
# SO THE OVER-REACH STAYS, AND IT IS BOUNDED IN TWO DIRECTIONS RATHER THAN
# HOPED ABOUT. An EXPLICIT `git add <path>` refuses out loud, names the path and
# offers `-f` — only `git add -A` is quiet — and anything force-added past that
# refusal is what case C above reds on. A regression fixture for this very rule
# would be named exactly like a moved-aside directory, which is why the guard
# builds every such name at run time and asks a throwaway repository: it owns no
# committed file its own subject rule would swallow.
#
# `tests/prevention/gitignore-anchoring.test.ts` reds if any directory rule in
# any tracked `.gitignore` stops being covered by this one, AND it drives BOTH
# candidates above — the narrowed single rule and the width-enumerated set —
# each to the specific failure named here rather than to a bare "it does not
# work". It also drives the widths at run time, so the claim that the deployed
# rule covers every one of them is re-measured on every run instead of being
# read. What that arm cannot do is search the language for a spelling nobody
# has proposed; the promise is bounded to the candidates it drives, which is
# the promise this paragraph makes and the one the previous version did not.
**/*-[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]*

# OS / editor noise, which appears wherever the file being edited is.
**/.DS_Store
**/*.swp

# Public-repository secret hygiene (added for the public snapshot)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
.npmrc
.netrc
**/.code-harness*
.bridlewise/
**/.bridlewise/
*.db
*.db-*
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo
test-results/
playwright-report/
coverage/
.impeccable/
docs/_site/
docs/.jekyll-cache/
